course: dictionary
lesson: 2292
title: Softer words for surprise
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words show a feeling rather than a thing. You can often tell from the words around them. «Crikey, that spider is huge!» The shout comes straight after a surprise, so crikey is a burst of feeling, like «wow!».
explain: Long ago, people did not like to say God's or Jesus's name in anger, so they changed the words into softer ones. «Gosh» and «golly» came from God, and «crikey» came from Christ. A softer stand-in like this is called a euphemism.
explain: «Crikey» is mostly heard in British and Australian English, and it sounds old-fashioned and friendly. An Aussie on a wildlife show might say, «Crikey, that croc is huge!» Even if you have never heard it before, the words around it show you it is surprise, not anger.
fact: Crikey is an interjection: a word that shouts out a feeling, like surprise or alarm.
fact: «Crikey» means a cry of surprise or alarm, like «wow!» or «goodness!».
fact: It began as a soft stand-in for «Christ», so people could avoid using that name in anger.
fact: It is mild, old-fashioned and mostly British and Australian, and it is safe in most company.
fact: Other softened words include «gosh», «golly», «gee» and «cripes».
exercise: «Crikey, that spider is huge!» What kind of word is crikey? | A verb meaning to run away | *An interjection, a shout of feeling | A place where spiders live | A noun for a type of spider
exercise: Crikey means a cry of surprise or alarm, like what? | Yes or no | *Wow or goodness | Please or thank you | Sorry
exercise: Which word is a softened stand-in for «Christ»? | Hooray | Golly | *Crikey | Ouch
test: Where is crikey mostly heard? | In France and Spain | Only in American films | Only in Scotland | *In Britain and Australia
test: What does a child shouting «crikey» most likely show? | They are fast asleep | They are going to sing | *They are surprised | They are very hungry
exam: Why did people say «gosh» or «crikey» instead of a stronger word? | Because they were too tired to finish speaking | To sound grand and important | Because the stronger word was against the law | *To avoid saying God's or Jesus's name in anger
